WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.089 --> 00:00:04.650
It's great to welcome to the program today,
Roger Hallam, who's a British environmental

00:00:04.650 --> 00:00:11.980
activist and cofounder of extinction rebellion,
a movement that uses nonviolent civil disobedience

00:00:11.980 --> 00:00:15.070
to compel government action on climate change.

00:00:15.070 --> 00:00:16.520
Roger, it's so great to have you on.

00:00:16.520 --> 00:00:17.740
I appreciate it.

00:00:17.740 --> 00:00:18.740
Yeah.

00:00:18.740 --> 00:00:19.740
Great.

00:00:19.740 --> 00:00:20.740
Thanks.

00:00:20.740 --> 00:00:24.750
So this is a topic I'm very interested in
because I've long argued that, uh, here in

00:00:24.750 --> 00:00:30.680
the United States, for example, calling your
elected official and signing a petition online

00:00:30.680 --> 00:00:35.920
is a very rarely going to really achieve the
kind of systemic change that we need in order

00:00:35.920 --> 00:00:38.960
to deal with some of the major issues that
are facing us.

00:00:38.960 --> 00:00:42.200
Climate change and environmental issues certainly
included.

00:00:42.200 --> 00:00:49.990
And I know that for me, violent civil disobedience
feels like it is over the line, but finding

00:00:49.990 --> 00:00:56.180
exactly where the line is, uh, is maybe more
complicated than, than, than some might think.

00:00:56.180 --> 00:00:57.180
So.

00:00:57.180 --> 00:01:01.110
Can you talk a little bit about your perspective
and some of the protests you've organized?

00:01:01.110 --> 00:01:07.020
Well, the first thing to say is, as I'm sure
people know, and the climate change crisis

00:01:07.020 --> 00:01:12.969
has been going on now for 30 years, since
1990 when scientists first told people that

00:01:12.969 --> 00:01:15.299
we're heading for this massive catastrophe.

00:01:15.299 --> 00:01:17.380
If something isn't, doesn't happen.

00:01:17.380 --> 00:01:22.109
So as you rightly say, people been sending
emails and lobbying and doing all the conventional

00:01:22.109 --> 00:01:28.750
stuff for 30 years and carbon emissions have
gone up by 60% which is beyond bad as everyone

00:01:28.750 --> 00:01:29.750
knows.

00:01:29.750 --> 00:01:35.070
So you know, extinction rebellion came along
where you with a blank sheet of paper a bit

00:01:35.070 --> 00:01:38.009
like what you've said and said, okay so that's
not working.

00:01:38.009 --> 00:01:42.709
So what needs to work and the fact of the
matter is is civil disobedience does work

00:01:42.709 --> 00:01:46.239
as long as it's constructed in a particular
way.

00:01:46.239 --> 00:01:51.840
And just to sort of expand on that, civil
disobedience is a way of peacefully breaking

00:01:51.840 --> 00:01:52.840
the law.

00:01:52.840 --> 00:01:55.509
They involves respect towards your opponent.

00:01:55.509 --> 00:01:59.079
So it's really a little bit complicated to
get your head around it cause obviously it

00:01:59.079 --> 00:02:01.909
disrupting people's lives and the government.

00:02:01.909 --> 00:02:06.259
But at the same time you're saying, look,
we want to talk and we want to sort this problem

00:02:06.259 --> 00:02:07.259
out.

00:02:07.259 --> 00:02:11.360
And that's really why extinction rebellion
is about and what civil disobedience is about.

00:02:11.360 --> 00:02:17.620
Can you talk a little bit about some of the
tactics and techniques that you use?

00:02:17.620 --> 00:02:25.840
Yeah, the main, the main way of disrupting
governments I guess is to sit in the road.

00:02:25.840 --> 00:02:30.180
Some people paint buildings, some people go
on hunger strike and none of this is what

00:02:30.180 --> 00:02:32.290
people want to do or hasten to white, right?

00:02:32.290 --> 00:02:37.079
It's not the sort of thing you'd do lightly
upon thousands of people around the world

00:02:37.079 --> 00:02:41.040
and now made that decision because the pennies
dropped that nothing else is working.

00:02:41.040 --> 00:02:44.349
And that's the thing that gets the disruption.

00:02:44.349 --> 00:02:45.859
So there's two things going on.

00:02:45.859 --> 00:02:50.360
You're disrupting people, but also people
are impressed by your sacrifice.

00:02:50.360 --> 00:02:54.109
People work, you know, getting arrested, going
to prison and what have you.

00:02:54.109 --> 00:02:56.170
And obviously no one does that lightly.

00:02:56.170 --> 00:03:01.590
So people tend, tend to think even if they
disagree with you, I think at least I go,

00:03:01.590 --> 00:03:05.739
well you know the guy is actually standing
by way believes.

00:03:05.739 --> 00:03:08.550
And so maybe there's something to what he's
saying.

00:03:08.550 --> 00:03:10.200
So that's really how it works.

00:03:10.200 --> 00:03:15.189
Do we have data as to what the public perception
is of some of these actions?

00:03:15.189 --> 00:03:18.689
Because, for example, in the United States,
we've had a number of different protests that

00:03:18.689 --> 00:03:24.140
involve shutting down a bridge, which is a
key commuting artery into or out of a city.

00:03:24.140 --> 00:03:29.709
And at least anecdotally, a lot of the reaction
seems to be I need to go to work.

00:03:29.709 --> 00:03:31.640
And if I'm not there, I get fired.

00:03:31.640 --> 00:03:33.930
I can no longer feed my kids.

00:03:33.930 --> 00:03:39.640
And it's, it's in a sense of violent act against
my ability to put food on my family's table.

00:03:39.640 --> 00:03:45.639
So I wonder, uh, what the reaction, do we
have anything empirical about how people do

00:03:45.639 --> 00:03:50.000
react when they hear about such a protest
that is if, for example, a blocking the road

00:03:50.000 --> 00:03:51.000
protest?

00:03:51.000 --> 00:03:55.069
Well, the first thing to say is, you know,
it's a bit of a no brainer.

00:03:55.069 --> 00:03:56.069
Obviously.

00:03:56.069 --> 00:03:58.579
It's totally maddening if someone's stopping
you getting to work.

00:03:58.579 --> 00:04:00.420
So no one's trying to dispute that.

00:04:00.420 --> 00:04:03.319
The point of civil disobedience is you're
taking responsibility.

00:04:03.319 --> 00:04:05.650
You're not trying to run away from your actions.

00:04:05.650 --> 00:04:07.819
It's not like you're blocking the road and
then running off.

00:04:07.819 --> 00:04:10.689
You're basically saying to people, I'm blocking
road.

00:04:10.689 --> 00:04:17.130
I know I'm acting illegally and I'm more than
prepared to go to court and you know, suffer

00:04:17.130 --> 00:04:18.739
for what I've done.

00:04:18.739 --> 00:04:22.670
I'm prepared to get fined, I'm prepared to
go to prison if needs be.

00:04:22.670 --> 00:04:24.330
So that changes things a little bit.

00:04:24.330 --> 00:04:30.419
But in terms of how it affects the public,
like what we need to look at is like the public

00:04:30.419 --> 00:04:31.580
as a whole.

00:04:31.580 --> 00:04:33.360
And I can give you an interesting stat.

00:04:33.360 --> 00:04:37.660
For instance, what happened last year, if
you want some specific data on it.

00:04:37.660 --> 00:04:40.821
Like most people in Britain aren't really
heard of the climate emergency.

00:04:40.821 --> 00:04:45.020
A bit like in America, everyone thought it
was a little bit of a small problem, but actually

00:04:45.020 --> 00:04:47.000
it's a massive problem.

00:04:47.000 --> 00:04:50.270
And 1,200 people were arrested last April.

00:04:50.270 --> 00:04:54.389
It was the biggest civil disobedience in British
history.

00:04:54.389 --> 00:05:01.340
And after that the episode 67% of the British
population agreed there was a climate emergency.

00:05:01.340 --> 00:05:02.340
In other words.

00:05:02.340 --> 00:05:06.990
So it was like tens of millions of conversations
in parts, you know, around kitchen tables

00:05:06.990 --> 00:05:11.599
where people are going, hang on a minute,
there's thousands of people getting arrested.

00:05:11.599 --> 00:05:12.599
This is a big issue.

00:05:12.599 --> 00:05:16.580
We sort of knew it was a big issue and now
we know it's a big issue and that's how attitude

00:05:16.580 --> 00:05:18.480
change works.

00:05:18.480 --> 00:05:22.840
So in other words, even if at the micro level
someone is prevented from getting to work

00:05:22.840 --> 00:05:27.590
as an example, if the social consciousness
rises to the point where you have critical

00:05:27.590 --> 00:05:32.190
mass towards action, you you consider it a
net benefit.

00:05:32.190 --> 00:05:35.509
That's, that's what happens on a social level.

00:05:35.509 --> 00:05:36.509
Yeah.

00:05:36.509 --> 00:05:38.100
But it has to be done in a respectful way.

00:05:38.100 --> 00:05:40.150
And this is the thing that people often forget.

00:05:40.150 --> 00:05:45.500
This isn't like just about rioting or about
being sort of aggressive and what have you.

00:05:45.500 --> 00:05:47.490
It's calm is ordered, it's respectful.

00:05:47.490 --> 00:05:53.629
And then as I said, even if disagree with
you, they certainly have a, you know, a third

00:05:53.629 --> 00:05:56.229
respect for you putting yourself on the line.

00:05:56.229 --> 00:06:01.729
And I think the other thing we need to understand
is that civil disobedience works, but it only

00:06:01.729 --> 00:06:04.210
works if you're doing something on an issue.

00:06:04.210 --> 00:06:05.210
That's right.

00:06:05.210 --> 00:06:09.260
You know, if I sit in a road and block, you're
getting to work and I want you to wear red

00:06:09.260 --> 00:06:13.270
hat for me at something, you're going to say,
I don't care that you're saying in the road,

00:06:13.270 --> 00:06:15.650
you know, no way am I going to do that for
you.

00:06:15.650 --> 00:06:19.159
It only really works when everyone knows it's
actually real.

00:06:19.159 --> 00:06:23.681
You know, this is objectively real, what's
happening with the climate crisis and this

00:06:23.681 --> 00:06:29.960
is the biggest problem is communicating that
we're absolutely in this dire situation and

00:06:29.960 --> 00:06:34.270
that's why people are taking this, you know,
dramatic step

00:06:34.270 --> 00:06:40.830
one of the tactics that seems to be more of
a gray area for different activists depending

00:06:40.830 --> 00:06:47.639
on where they draw the line is the destruction
of, for example, imagine that you're protesting

00:06:47.639 --> 00:06:53.689
logging going in the middle of the night so
nobody gets hurt and disabling or damaging

00:06:53.689 --> 00:06:55.259
bulldozers as an example.

00:06:55.259 --> 00:06:59.689
It's destruction of property, certainly it's
certainly a crime, but no one is being physically

00:06:59.689 --> 00:07:00.689
hurt.

00:07:00.689 --> 00:07:04.060
Where is something like that in your sort
of paradigm?

00:07:04.060 --> 00:07:09.849
Well, to be honest with you, like the old
sort of way of doing direct action as far

00:07:09.849 --> 00:07:13.490
as I'm concerned, and I've done five years
of research at King's college in London on

00:07:13.490 --> 00:07:19.310
it, the main way to change societies through
mass participation in civil disobedience,

00:07:19.310 --> 00:07:24.379
which means like tens of thousands, even hundreds
of thousands of people breaking the law in

00:07:24.379 --> 00:07:30.150
a small way in urban centers where everyone
can see them, where that's where the government

00:07:30.150 --> 00:07:32.139
is, what's the media and what happier.

00:07:32.139 --> 00:07:36.000
In other words, you go to the capital city
and this sort of thing has been happening

00:07:36.000 --> 00:07:40.319
in, in many countries of the world for the
last 50 years, particularly in the global

00:07:40.319 --> 00:07:45.860
South where you know, you've probably seen
a psychic Algeria or Sudan or many other countries.

00:07:45.860 --> 00:07:49.449
People go to the capital city and say, we
don't want to live under a dictatorship on

00:07:49.449 --> 00:07:54.419
the corruption anymore, and we're sitting
down until we get some semblance of democracy.

00:07:54.419 --> 00:07:59.500
So we're really, we're borrowing that methodology
from those people and applying it to Western

00:07:59.500 --> 00:08:05.750
democracies where we have this corrupt situation
of governments listening to fossil fuel companies

00:08:05.750 --> 00:08:10.960
a lot more to ordinary citizens who are concerned
about their children's future.

00:08:10.960 --> 00:08:15.280
So that's really what we're aiming for, rather
than, you know, 10 people doing something

00:08:15.280 --> 00:08:16.850
in the middle of the night as it were.

00:08:16.850 --> 00:08:21.490
So in other words, it's less about figuring
out whether destruction of property is on

00:08:21.490 --> 00:08:25.850
this side or that side of the line, but recognizing
that that's too small and action compared

00:08:25.850 --> 00:08:31.050
to the mass rally at an urban center to really
achieve the, the sort of, uh, attention that

00:08:31.050 --> 00:08:33.630
you believe is necessary to get change to
happen.

00:08:33.630 --> 00:08:34.630
Yeah.

00:08:34.630 --> 00:08:38.520
And it's not, to be honest with you, primarily
about the physical disruption.

00:08:38.520 --> 00:08:41.230
It's more about the emotional communication.

00:08:41.230 --> 00:08:46.970
You know, when you see someone who's 84 or
a kid who's 14 getting dragged off by the

00:08:46.970 --> 00:08:47.970
police, they're not, no.

00:08:47.970 --> 00:08:52.450
It immediately has a emotional reaction, you
know, whether you are opposed to it or not,

00:08:52.450 --> 00:08:54.240
you're thinking, what's going on here?

00:08:54.240 --> 00:08:55.240
You know?

00:08:55.240 --> 00:08:57.990
And when normal people come out in the streets,
and this is what it's all about.

00:08:57.990 --> 00:09:01.440
It's not about activists or other, you know,
I'm just, I'm a farmer.

00:09:01.440 --> 00:09:07.751
I've been doing growing food for people for
30 years now and it's normal people that are

00:09:07.751 --> 00:09:11.780
starting to take the initiative here rather
than what you might call the old activist

00:09:11.780 --> 00:09:12.780
communities.

00:09:12.780 --> 00:09:16.460
And that's the difference with extinction
rebellion is it's bringing up people from

00:09:16.460 --> 00:09:21.970
lots of different politics and cultural backgrounds,
you know, farmers, small business people,

00:09:21.970 --> 00:09:25.772
you know, single mothers, uh, you know, people
from all different backgrounds to saying,

00:09:25.772 --> 00:09:31.340
hang on a minute, this is a universal issue,
which affects everything that I believe in.

00:09:31.340 --> 00:09:34.230
You know, whether you've got right wing values
or left wing values, right.

00:09:34.230 --> 00:09:37.040
No one wants to see the destruction of America.

00:09:37.040 --> 00:09:38.040
Right.

00:09:38.040 --> 00:09:39.040
Is that right?

00:09:39.040 --> 00:09:40.040
I think so.

00:09:40.040 --> 00:09:41.040
For the most part.

00:09:41.040 --> 00:09:42.040
I think that's true.

00:09:42.040 --> 00:09:43.040
Yeah.

00:09:43.040 --> 00:09:46.000
And that's why I'm coming to America to do,
you know, I'm promoting my book.

00:09:46.000 --> 00:09:47.450
Um, common sense.

00:09:47.450 --> 00:09:52.890
It's called common sense because it's related
to, um, Thomas Payne.

00:09:52.890 --> 00:09:56.810
I don't know if you know Thomas bang, but
he came to America and the American revolution,

00:09:56.810 --> 00:10:01.650
he said to Americans, you know, you're not
going to get rid of the British unless you

00:10:01.650 --> 00:10:03.650
just have a revolution inside.

00:10:03.650 --> 00:10:05.520
No, we don't want the British crown anymore.

00:10:05.520 --> 00:10:11.680
And what I'm saying to the American public
is, you need to deal with this directly by

00:10:11.680 --> 00:10:15.620
having thousands of people on the street saying,
we don't actually want the destruction of

00:10:15.620 --> 00:10:18.910
our families or communities on the American
nation.

00:10:18.910 --> 00:10:21.370
That's fundamentally what this is about.

00:10:21.370 --> 00:10:22.370
No question.

00:10:22.370 --> 00:10:27.250
And we've been talking about, uh, the importance
of, of mass rallies that urban centers as

00:10:27.250 --> 00:10:30.520
a tool for achieving change, uh, for, for
years on the program.

00:10:30.520 --> 00:10:35.900
Uh, Roger Hallam is a British environmental
activist, cofounder of extinction, rebellion

00:10:35.900 --> 00:10:36.900
office

00:10:36.900 --> 00:10:37.900
there of common sense.

00:10:37.900 --> 00:10:40.790
Roger's such a pleasure having you on and
I appreciate you sharing your perspective.

00:10:40.790 --> 00:10:41.790
Yeah.

00:10:41.790 --> 00:10:42.790
Well thank you.

00:10:42.790 --> 00:10:43.660
Good luck

